      "Language": "fre",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "The presentation of cystitis associated with lumbago in a young woman lead to the discovery on IVU of left pyelitic irregularities. Ultrasound demonstrated a non-homogeneous lateral pyelitic mass. Because we suspected a vascular aetiology, we performed arteriography: arteries were normal, the renal vein was not visualized, but opacities caused by peripyelitic varices being drained by a much dilated lumbo-ovarian vein which gave a normal intramyometrial phlebographic picture, were seen. Selective catheterization of the renal vein was not possible to perform. Was this then a case of agenesis or thrombosis? Tomography didn't help decide. The persistence of severe pain contraindicated lumbotomy, and the presence of a wide but flat and impermeable renal vein. A complete denervation of the kidney was carried out. The normal artery and the ovarian vein were preserved intact. The follow-up was straight forward-routine IVU was normal. The pains disappeared."},
